[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue and marked known. Its reports held more than
one problem. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: the parser is fixed, and the `booking-v3` form
is retired instead of fixed. Each problem has its own ticket below, with where it stands.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 was not one issue. The parser fix closes only the parser reports.
</Callout>

## What #4471 held

<SplitTable
  ticket={4471}
  tickets={{
    parser: { href: 'https://github.com/codingpains/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1', state: 'fixed' },
    hint: { href: 'https://github.com/codingpains/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2', state: "won't fix: form retired" },
  }}
  oneDay={{ href: 'https://github.com/codingpains/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/3', state: 'open' }}
/>

- **`parser`**: the shared parser read every date month first. Every tenant on file writes the
  day first, so day and month swapped. Fixed: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.
- **`hint`**: the `booking-v3` form printed `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, people typed the
  month first, and the form read the date day first. Only bookings typed into that form, at the
  tenants who ran it. The parser fix does not reach it. Won't fix: the form is retired.
- **one-day move**: the date moves by one day; day and month do not swap. The desk read these as a
  one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) and files them under the parser by tenant, so they count there
  too. Neither the parser fix nor the form's retirement moves a date back by a day. A meeting in
  Lisbon and Toronto landing on different days points at time zones; not confirmed.

## How it was counted

- One id, one report. The export repeats some reports because its pages overlap; they count once.
- A report goes where its **tenant** puts it: the locale they write dates in and the booking form
  they run (`causes.ts`), as the desk counts. The intake tag is a guess from the reporter's
  browser: it is shown, and used for nothing.
- The table is built from the export at render time, so the numbers follow the data.

## Next time

- Matching on subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) put every
  problem in one ticket: the subjects match, the causes do not. Before merging, check that the
  tenant and the way the report came in point to the same cause.
- When a fix ships, watch for reports that keep coming: they belong to a problem the fix did not cover.

